AB  - The microservices architectures have turned out to be the foundation of the modern cloud-native software systems because they are scalable, flexible, and allow continuous deployment. Nevertheless, the growing architectural sophistication of microservices presents serious obstacles to the tradeoffs assessment of key quality features, including performance, scalability, availability, reliability, and modifiability. The Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method (ATAM) is a methodical way of assessing architectural designs, but it relies extensively on qualitative professional judgments, so it cannot be repeated or scaled when operating in dynamic contexts. An extension of ATAM is presented in this paper, where machine learning techniques are employed to facilitate automated risk assessment for microservice systems. An all-encompassing information set of 2000 microservices architecture scenarios was created through the incorporation of the architectural structure indicators, performance indicators at runtime, resource utilization indicators, fault behavior, and scalability features. Several architectural risk predictive machine learning algorithms, such as the Logistic Regression, Support Vector Machine, Gradient Boosting, Random Forest, K-Nearest Neighbors, Naive Bayes, and Decision Tree, were developed and tested to predict the level of architectural risk measured as Low, Medium, and High. The suggested framework will turn ATAM into a scalable, quantitative, and data-oriented decision-support framework that fits the contemporary cloudnative microservice environments. The code is available on the GitHub repository: https://github.com/mldlwork2026/ATAMML
ER  - 

