AB  - The growing reliance on Machine Learning (ML) applications across industries has increased the need for operational practices that ensure scalable, automated, and reproducible model deployment. Although Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipelines are well established in traditional software engineering, their direct application to ML workflows remains challenging due to the non-deterministic and data-dependent nature of ML models. Existing Machine Learning Operations (MLOps) tools such as Kubeflow, MLflow, and TFX provide partial automation but do not offer a unified framework capable of supporting the full ML lifecycle, including data versioning, iterative retraining, monitoring, and governance.This research investigates current CI/CD practices within ML systems to identify limitations in scalability, traceability, interoperability, and lifecycle automation. To address this gap, mixed-method research was conducted, including an intensive review of 33 recent scholarly studies and using empirical insights from a 70-participant survey. The research synthesizes the operational and governance requirements needed for effective ML lifecycle management. As a result of these investigations, the paper proposes the conceptual foundations of a standardized CI/CD framework tailored specifically to ML systems. The proposed framework emphasizes automation, reproducibility, observability, and seamless integration across data, model, and deployment pipelines. Finally, the findings suggest that adopting such a framework can significantly enhance ML operational reliability, particularly within emerging AI ecosystems such as Bahrain.
ER  - 

