AB  - Wildlife monitoring increasingly employs artificial intelligence (AI) for automated detection, classification, and tracking in natural habitats. While cloud-based approaches provide strong computational resources, they face latency, connectivity, and energy limitations in remote areas. Edge computing addresses these constraints by enabling local inference on distributed devices, reducing bandwidth demand and supporting real-time analytics. This survey reviews deployment and design strategies for AI-powered edge systems in wildlife monitoring, with focus on standalone, edge–cloud and federated architectures. We further examine containerization, orchestration frameworks, and continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) practices in distributed environments. Specific attention is given to device heterogeneity, energy management, and long-term reliability under field conditions. Finally, we outline open challenges and research directions toward scalable, sustainable and autonomous wildlife monitoring with edge AI.
