AB  - The increasing adoption of Internet of Things (IoT) technologies in Water Distribution Networks (WDNs) enables real-time monitoring and predictive analytics but introduces significant challenges in managing Machine Learning (ML) models across distributed and heterogeneous environments. This paper presents an MLOps-based framework for the continuous deployment and operation of ML models in IoTenabled WDNs, leveraging a cloud-edge architecture aligned with LoRaWAN infrastructures. The proposed approach integrates CI/CD pipelines, containerization, automated model versioning, and runtime monitoring into a closed-loop system that supports adaptive and conditionaware model management. Predictive models based on Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks are deployed at the edge to forecast key hydraulic variables such as tank levels and pressure. Model activation, updating, and retraining are dynamically driven by event-based and performance-based triggers. The framework is evaluated in an emulation environment using EPANET and WNTR simulations, demonstrating effective deployment, scalability, and accurate forecasting performance while highlighting the trade-off between model complexity and computational efficiency.
